# SignupForm — name, email, password, submit. `LoginForm`'s sibling, and the
# differences between them are the reason it is a second component rather than a
# flag on the first.
#
#     state name: text = "" @client
#     state email: text = "" @client
#     state pw: text = "" @client
#     state pwShown: bool = false @client
#
#     use AuthCard("Create an account", "Free while it is in beta."):
#         use SignupForm(register, "Create account", name, email, pw, pwShown,
#                        "At least 12 characters.")
#
# ── THE ACTION TAKES THREE ARGUMENTS, IN THIS ORDER ──────────────────────────
#
#     form "{submitLabel}" -> a(name, email, password)
#
# `a` must be an action of exactly that arity and order; the compiler checks it at
# the call site, so a mismatch is a build failure rather than a form that posts
# the email into the name column. See `auth/loginform.fct` for why a component can
# invoke an action with arguments at all — it is because these three cells are its
# own reference parameters, and a general-purpose submit component still cannot.
#
#     action register(name: text, email: text, password: text):
#         check name != "" "tell us what to call you"
#         check contains(email, "@") "that does not look like an email address"
#         check !exists(u in Account where u.email == email) "that address is already registered"
#         add Account { name: name, email: email, ... }
#
# ── WHY IT IS NOT `LoginForm` WITH AN EXTRA FIELD ────────────────────────────
#
# Three things differ and each of them is the difference between a form that works
# and one that irritates. It has a name field, so a three-argument action. Its
# password field carries a `passwordHint` — the rule, stated BEFORE the reader
# types rather than after they are rejected, which is the whole argument for
# `hint` existing alongside `error` in `forms/fieldmessage.fct`. And it has a slot
# under the fields, because a sign-up is the one form that reliably has something
# to say before you press it: terms, a marketing opt-in (`CheckboxField` on the
# caller's own cell), a note about what happens next.
#
# ── THERE IS NO CONFIRM-PASSWORD FIELD ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# Not an omission. A confirmation box needs its own cell and a check that the two
# match, and the check has to run on the AUTHORITY to mean anything — where only
# one of the two values ever arrives, because the second is never sent. So the
# comparison would have to be a client-side `if` in the view, which is a
# validation this library will not pretend to: a form that says "passwords do not
# match" from a rule the server never sees is a form that will one day accept a
# mismatch, and typing it twice with no working reveal (see below) buys nothing a
# password manager does not already do better. The mitigation is letting the box
# accept a manager's fill and generated value in the first place, which is what
# `NewPasswordField` is for.
#
# ── THE PASSWORD BOX IS `NewPasswordField`, NOT `PasswordField` ──────────────
#
# `auth/passwordfield.fct` names both: `PasswordField` writes the
# `current-password` autocomplete token, which tells a manager to fill the
# account's EXISTING secret — correct for sign-in, wrong here, because a
# sign-up box should get the OTHER token, `new-password`, so a manager offers
# to GENERATE one instead. `NewPasswordField` is that second component; a
# control keyword is not an expression, so one component cannot pick the token
# at runtime and there has to be two. Read that file before reaching for either
# one elsewhere.
#
# `SignupForm` still takes a `shown: cell bool` parameter and no longer uses
# it: `NewPasswordField` carries no reveal (see `auth/passwordfield.fct` for
# why one is not expressible against a real password control), so there is
# nothing left for it to drive. It stays in this signature only because a
# library does not change a component's arity out from under a caller — see
# the identical trade-off `PasswordField` documents for the same parameter.
import "../forms/form.fct"
import "../forms/textfield.fct"
import "passwordfield.fct"
import "loginform.fct"
app FacetAuth:
    component SignupForm(a: action, submitLabel: text, name: cell text, email: cell text, password: cell text, shown: cell bool, passwordHint: text):
        box class "x-authform":
            use Form(a):
                form "{submitLabel}" -> a(name, email, password):
                    use TextField("Name", "Ada Lovelace", true, "", "", name)
                    use TextField("Email", "you@example.com", true, "", "", email)
                    use NewPasswordField("Password", "", true, passwordHint, failed(a), password)
                    slot
